% ── Abstract ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{abstract}
\noindent
ProofLayer is a two-stage system for screening government cybersecurity policy
documents against control catalogues: a keyword stage that maps policy text to
a twenty-two control set crosswalked to NIST SP 800-53 Revision~5, and a
language-model stage intended to resolve the cases keyword matching cannot,
with a cost-aware orchestration layer routing between models. This paper
evaluates the first stage on ten Maryland state and local government policy
documents and separates what is measured from what is only described. Reading
each document in full gives a mean control coverage of 62.8 percent
(standard deviation 24.1). An earlier analysis of this corpus truncated each
document at fifteen pages and reported 49.1 percent; seven of the ten documents
exceed fifteen pages, and the two longest gain 36 and 41 points when read in
full. Both figures are reported, because a screening metric that moves this far
with extraction depth is itself informative. Coverage correlates with document
length at $r=0.690$ against log word count, so 48 percent of its variance is
explained by length alone. Of 138 covered document-control pairs, 18.1 percent
carry a negation cue within 130 characters of the match and 44.2 percent rest
on a single keyword out of a mean 6.1 available. A worked error analysis gives
four cases, among them a footnote URL scored as transport encryption and an
incident-reporting requirement carrying a two-business-day deadline scored as
absent because the keyword list lacked the word \emph{notifying}. The
second-stage classifier is described but not evaluated: establishing whether it
resolves these failures requires expert-annotated ground truth for this corpus,
which does not yet exist, and model-generated labels are declined as a
substitute. The orchestration cost saving is a token-price projection rather
than instrumented spend. We therefore report policy-vocabulary coverage, not
compliance, and release all 220 document-control pairs with matched keywords
and text snippets.
\end{abstract}

\vspace{0.6em}
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} cybersecurity policy analysis; control coverage
screening; NIST SP 800-53; government cybersecurity; keyword matching;
measurement validity; large language models; Maryland

\newpage

% ── 1. Introduction ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Introduction}

State and local governments are increasingly targeted by sophisticated cyberattacks.
Between 2022 and 2025, over 1,800 reported breaches affected public and private
organizations in the United States, with ransomware attacks on Baltimore City costing
an estimated \$18 million and similar incidents shutting down services across Maryland
school systems and healthcare networks~\cite{md_cyber_council_2025}. Despite
growing awareness of these threats, a 2021 analysis by the Maryland Cybersecurity
Council found that 53.8\% of local emergency management offices lacked a consequence
management plan, and only 31\% of local school systems reported adequate cybersecurity
budget allocations~\cite{md_local_gov_2021}.

Federal compliance frameworks such as NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 5
\cite{nist800_53_2020}, the Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program
(FedRAMP)~\cite{fedramp_2022}, and the Cybersecurity Maturity Model Certification
(CMMC)~\cite{cmmc_2022} provide structured sets of security controls for assessing
organizational cybersecurity posture. However, mapping government policy documents to
these frameworks manually is prohibitively expensive: independent assessments for
FedRAMP authorization can cost between \$50,000 and \$500,000 and take 6--18 months
to complete~\cite{fedramp_2022}. This cost burden falls disproportionately on smaller
agencies and municipalities that have limited IT security budgets.

Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) open a new possibility: automating
the reading, interpretation, and gap analysis of free-text policy documents against
structured control frameworks. Prior work has demonstrated LLM applicability to
legal compliance analysis~\cite{hassani_2024}, post-incident policy gap
detection~\cite{policy_gap_llm_2026}, multi-framework regulatory gap
detection~\cite{compliancenlp_2026}, and compliance automation in financial
services~\cite{rethink_compliance_nlp_2025}. However, no prior work has applied
LLM-based policy screening specifically to state and local government
cybersecurity policy documents at scale, nor has the cost efficiency of a
multi-LLM routing strategy been evaluated in this domain. This paper addresses
the first gap only, and for the keyword stage alone; the routing strategy is
described here but its cost efficiency remains unevaluated.

This paper makes one evaluated contribution and two descriptive ones, and we
distinguish them explicitly because only the first rests on measurement.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Evaluated.} An \textbf{empirical keyword-based control coverage
  mapping} of ten Maryland state and local government cybersecurity policy documents
  against 22 controls crosswalked to 13 NIST SP 800-53 Revision~5 families, reporting
  a mean coverage of $62.8\%$ on full text, together with a characterisation of how
  far that figure is driven by document length, negation and single-keyword matches,
  and a worked error analysis. This is the result the paper stands on.

  \item \textbf{Described, not evaluated.} The design and implementation of
  \textbf{ProofLayer}, a policy screening pipeline that ingests policy documents
  and produces structured audit reports. Its second stage applies an LLM to gap
  analysis and remediation guidance. That stage is implemented and specified here,
  but it has not been evaluated against expert annotation, so no accuracy claim is
  made for it. Section~\ref{sec:limitations} states the annotation protocol that
  would settle the question.

  \item \textbf{Projected, not measured.} A \textbf{cost-aware multi-LLM routing
  strategy} that directs cheaper models to extraction and stronger models to
  reasoning. An arithmetic model over published vendor rates projects roughly 24\%
  lower per-audit inference cost than using a single high-capability model
  throughout. This is a projection from list prices, not an observed saving, and it
  inherits whatever error the underlying token estimates carry.
\end{enumerate}

Separating these matters for how the paper should be read. A two-stage system
whose second stage is unevaluated is, evidentially, a one-stage study with an
architecture proposal attached, and we present it as such rather than letting the
system diagram carry an implied claim.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~2 reviews related work.
Section~3 describes the ProofLayer system architecture. Section~4 presents our
methodology. Section~5 reports experimental results on the Maryland policy corpus.
Section~6 discusses findings and limitations. Section~7 concludes.


% ── 2. Background and Related Work ───────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Background and Related Work}

This section provides the context needed to situate ProofLayer within existing work.
We first survey the federal and state cybersecurity compliance frameworks against which
policy documents are audited, then review recent applications of LLMs to regulatory
and compliance analysis, and finally examine the emerging literature on multi-LLM
orchestration for cost-aware AI pipelines.

\subsection{Cybersecurity Compliance Frameworks}

NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5~\cite{nist800_53_2020} is the canonical reference for
federal information system security controls, defining over 1,000 security controls and control enhancements across
20 families covering areas from access control (AC) and audit accountability (AU)
to system and communications protection (SC). FedRAMP builds on NIST 800-53 to
specify cloud security requirements for federal agency cloud services~\cite{fedramp_2022}.
CMMC extends this framework to DoD supply chain contractors, defining three
maturity levels comprising 110 practices mapped from NIST SP 800-171~\cite{cmmc_2022}.

Maryland has aligned its state Minimum Cybersecurity Standards to the NIST
Cybersecurity Framework~\cite{md_doit_web}, and since 2022 has required all units of
local government to certify compliance with state minimum standards under SB754
of 2022~\cite{md_local_gov_2021}. Nevertheless, the 2021 Maryland Cybersecurity
Council analysis found wide variation in policy coverage and implementation across
agencies, particularly at the local government level.

\subsection{LLMs for Compliance and Regulatory Analysis}

The intersection of natural language processing and regulatory compliance has been
an active research area. Early work used rule-based and classical machine learning
methods to extract compliance obligations from regulatory text~\cite{compliancenlp_2026}.
More recently, LLMs have shown strong performance on compliance-related tasks including
legal document interpretation, privacy policy analysis, and policy gap
detection~\cite{motlagh_2024}. Salman et al.~\cite{salman_2024} specifically examine
LLMs for cybersecurity compliance, showing that continuous LLM-based monitoring can
reduce reliance on expensive periodic audits.

Hassani et al.~\cite{hassani_2024} argue that LLMs enable a shift from sentence-level
classification to end-to-end compliance reasoning, addressing a key limitation of prior
NLP approaches that processed policy text in isolated fragments. Their work on legal
compliance automation at the IEEE Requirements Engineering conference provides a
framework for thinking about how LLMs can handle the full context of a regulatory
document. Independently, the ``Automated Post-Incident Policy Gap Analysis'' system
by Oh et al.~\cite{policy_gap_llm_2026} demonstrates an LLM-driven multi-agent
workflow that maps system event log evidence to MITRE ATT\&CK entries and then
evaluates organizational policies for adequacy, using \texttt{GPT-4o} and LangGraph.
ComplianceNLP~\cite{compliancenlp_2026} combines knowledge-graph-augmented
retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with multi-task obligation extraction to achieve
87.7 F1 on gap detection across SEC, MiFID II, and Basel III regulatory frameworks,
outperforming \texttt{GPT-4o}+RAG baselines by 3.5 F1 points.

Our work differs from the above in three ways: (i) we target government cybersecurity
policy documents rather than financial regulations; (ii) we apply keyword-based Stage 1
mapping as a no-API-cost baseline before engaging LLM reasoning; and (iii) we evaluate
the cost efficiency of multi-model routing, a concern that prior work has not addressed
in the compliance context.

\subsection{Multi-LLM Orchestration}

The idea of routing tasks to different LLMs based on complexity and cost is an emerging
research direction. xRouter~\cite{xrouter_2025} trains a reinforcement learning router
that routes requests to cost-appropriate models under explicit economic constraints,
achieving strong Pareto-optimal operating points on cost-accuracy tradeoffs. Benchmarking
by Kulkarni and Kulkarni~\cite{multillm_bench_2026} compares sequential, parallel, hierarchical,
and reflexive multi-agent architectures for financial document processing, finding that
reflexive architectures achieve the highest field-level accuracy but at 2.3$\times$ the
cost of sequential baselines. ProofLayer applies analogous routing principles to the compliance auditing
domain: lightweight models for pattern recognition and text extraction, stronger models
for reasoning-intensive gap analysis and report generation.


% ── 3. System Architecture ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{System Architecture}
\label{sec:arch}

Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} illustrates the ProofLayer architecture, which consists
of five components: a document ingestion layer, an AI extraction module, a control
mapping engine, a multi-LLM orchestration layer, and an output generation module.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1_architecture.pdf}
  \caption{ProofLayer system architecture. Policy documents enter through the ingestion
  layer, are processed by a two-stage compliance mapping pipeline, and routed to
  cost-appropriate LLMs for gap analysis and report generation. Source: generated by the authors from the analysis pipeline described in Section~\ref{sec:corpus}.}
  \label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Document Ingestion}

{\sloppy
ProofLayer accepts policy documents in PDF or plain text format.
PDF text is extracted using \texttt{pdfplumber}~\cite{pdfplumber},
which handles multi-column layouts common in government documents.
Extracted text is stored in memory with a unique document ID for subsequent processing.
Documents exceeding 15,000~characters are flagged for routing to
\texttt{claude-sonnet}, which offers strong long-context performance at competitive
cost relative to token count. Character count serves as a practical proxy for token
count (approximately 4~characters per English token); at this threshold a document's
content occupies roughly 3,750 tokens, which approaches the practical input budget
available to \texttt{gpt-4o} after accounting for prompt template overhead, making
the larger effective context window of \texttt{claude-sonnet} beneficial for full-document coverage.
\par}

\subsection{AI Extraction}

An optional AI extraction pass uses \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} to normalize and summarize
security-relevant content from the raw document text, producing a condensed representation
of security practices, policies, and procedures. This step costs approximately \$0.003
per document (under 2,000 input tokens) and reduces noise for downstream processing.

\subsection{Control Mapping Engine}

The control mapping engine is the analytical core of ProofLayer. It implements a
deliberate two-stage pipeline that separates low-cost pattern recognition from
high-cost contextual reasoning, allowing the system to scale economically to large
document corpora while preserving analytical depth for controls that require it.

\textbf{Stage 1 -- Keyword Matching.} Each document is scanned for occurrence of
framework-specific keywords associated with each control in the dataset. This stage
requires no LLM API calls, runs in under 50 milliseconds per document, and incurs
zero LLM inference cost. Controls are marked as ``present'' if any of their associated keywords
appear in the document text.

\textbf{Stage 2 -- LLM Gap Analysis.} A structured prompt containing the document
summary and the full control list is sent to a reasoning-capable LLM (\texttt{GPT-4o} by
default, with optional routing to Claude Sonnet for long documents or Ollama/LLaMA-3
for free local operation). The model is asked to classify each control as \textit{verified},
\textit{partial}, or \textit{missing} and to provide an explanatory note. The JSON
response is parsed and merged with Stage 1 results.

\subsection{Control Dataset}

The ProofLayer control dataset contains 22 controls spanning 13 NIST SP 800-53 Rev~5
control families organized into ten category groupings, cross-referenced to their
FedRAMP, CMMC 2.0, and SOC2 equivalents. Each
control record includes a human-readable name, a set of associated keywords, the
applicable framework mappings, a risk level (HIGH or MEDIUM), and a remediation
recommendation. Table~\ref{tab:controls} summarizes the control families included.

