\subsection{Multi-LLM Orchestration Layer}

ProofLayer implements a cost-aware task routing strategy shown in
Table~\ref{tab:routing}. The routing decision is made per task type and optionally
per content length, directing extraction and keyword-mapping tasks to inexpensive
small models and reserving high-capability models for reasoning-intensive gap
analysis and report generation. When internet connectivity is unavailable or API
budget constraints are binding, the system falls back to a locally hosted
Ollama/LLaMA-3 instance, ensuring that auditing capability remains fully functional
in air-gapped or cost-constrained environments without any code changes.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{ProofLayer multi-LLM routing strategy.}
\label{tab:routing}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
\textbf{Task} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Approx. Cost/1K tok.} & \textbf{Rationale} \\
\midrule
Doc. Extraction       & \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}   & \$0.00015 & Fast, cheap          \\
Keyword/Sem. Mapping  & \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}   & \$0.00015 & Sufficient for matching \\
Gap Analysis          & \texttt{gpt-4o}        & \$0.005   & Needs strong reasoning \\
Report Generation     & \texttt{gpt-4o}        & \$0.005   & Structured output required \\
Long docs ($>$15K ch.)& \texttt{claude-sonnet} & \$0.003   & Strong long-context     \\
Offline/Free mode     & \texttt{ollama/llama3} & \$0.000   & Local, no API cost      \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Output Generation}

ProofLayer generates three types of outputs: (i) a structured JSON audit result
containing per-control status, framework scores, and gap details; (ii) an LLM-generated
narrative executive summary suitable for submission to an ISSO or inspector general;
and (iii) a downloadable PDF audit report produced using ReportLab~\cite{reportlab},
containing score summaries, framework breakdowns, gap tables, and remediation guidance.
An API endpoint at \texttt{/fix} additionally generates full policy document
language for missing controls on demand, enabling agencies to obtain draft policy
text as a remediation starting point without requiring specialized compliance
writing expertise.


% ── 4. Materials and Methods ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Materials and Methods}

This section describes the evaluation design used to assess ProofLayer on real
Maryland government cybersecurity policy documents. We detail the composition of
the document corpus, the keyword-based Stage 1 mapping procedure applied in this
study, the compliance scoring formula, and the cost estimation approach used to
compare multi-LLM routing against a single-model baseline.

\subsection{Policy Document Corpus}
\label{sec:corpus}

We evaluate ProofLayer on ten Maryland government cybersecurity policy documents
listed in Table~\ref{tab:corpus}. Documents were selected according to three criteria: (i) publicly available from
official Maryland government sources, (ii) primary focus on cybersecurity or
information security policy content, and (iii) coverage of a distinct agency type
or function. To the best of our knowledge, no publicly available Maryland cybersecurity
policy documents satisfying these criteria were excluded. The corpus spans major branches
and agencies including executive branch IT policy (DoIT), judicial branch,
education (MSDE), health (DHMH), and local government analysis
(UMGC/Maryland Cybersecurity Council). The corpus represents publicly available
Maryland policy documents and is not intended to be statistically representative
of all government cybersecurity policies.

\textbf{Relationship to prior work on the same jurisdiction.} These ten documents
are the cybersecurity-policy subset of a larger 51-document Maryland corpus
assembled for an earlier study of document-level classification~\cite{trivedi_ccsce_2026}.
That study asked a different question at a different granularity: whether a given
document is cybersecurity-relevant at all, evaluated against the six core functions
of the NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, comparing keyword matching to LLM-assisted
classification. It found keyword matching to have high recall but poor precision at
the document level. The present study takes the ten documents that survive that
relevance filter and asks a control-level question instead: which of 22 specific
NIST SP 800-53 controls each document addresses. The frameworks differ (CSF 2.0
against SP 800-53 Rev 5), the unit of analysis differs (document against
document-control pair), and no result from the earlier study is restated here as
new. We note the overlap explicitly because the corpora share a jurisdiction and
provenance, and because the earlier finding that keyword matching is imprecise at
the document level is part of why the present control-level results are framed as
indicators of likely gaps rather than confirmed deficiencies.

