\subsection{Stage 1: Keyword-Based Control Coverage Mapping}

For the empirical evaluation reported in this paper, we apply the Stage 1
keyword-based control mapping pipeline exclusively, since it provides a reproducible,
deterministic baseline that requires no LLM API keys and enables full replication of
results. The 22-control keyword dictionary was developed by the first author by mapping each
control's formal NIST definition and associated assessment procedures to domain-specific
keyword phrases (e.g., ``encryption at rest,'' ``disk encryption,'' ``aes,'' ``data at rest''
for PL-15); each control maps to between 3 and 15 keywords. The full keyword dictionary
is provided in the supplementary notebook. A control is marked as \textit{present} if
any associated keyword appears (case-insensitively) in the document text. Prompt lengths
were estimated from representative policy documents in the corpus.

\subsection{Scoring}
\label{sec:scoring}

To produce comparable, interpretable results across documents of varying length and
scope, we assign each document a single control coverage score derived from the
fraction of the 22 ProofLayer controls that are marked present. This score is then
mapped to a risk tier consistent with NIST maturity guidance. The overall compliance
coverage score for each document is computed as:
\begin{equation}
S = \left\lfloor \frac{|\text{matched controls}|}{|\text{total controls}|} \times 100 \right\rfloor
\label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
Documents are classified as \textbf{LOW} risk ($S \geq 80$), \textbf{MEDIUM} risk
($55 \leq S < 80$), or \textbf{HIGH} risk ($S < 55$) based on heuristic thresholds
calibrated to approximate NIST maturity guidance; these specific percentage values
are not prescribed by NIST SP 800-53 directly. The gap frequency for each control is the number of
documents in which that control is absent, providing a measure of systemic weakness
across the policy corpus.

\subsection{Cost Estimation (projected, not measured)}
\label{sec:cost}
\label{sec:cost}

LLM inference costs are estimated using published per-thousand-token pricing for each
model as of mid-2025. For a standard audit (document extraction, keyword mapping, LLM
gap analysis, and narrative report generation), we compare the ProofLayer multi-LLM
strategy against a single-model baseline in which all tasks are routed to
\texttt{gpt-4o}. Estimated input and output token counts are based on ProofLayer's
production prompt templates and typical policy document lengths (extraction: 2,000
input / 400 output tokens; mapping: 1,500 / 300; gap analysis: 3,000 / 700; report:
2,500 / 800; long-document routing via claude-sonnet: 6,700 / 1,000). These are model inputs, not
measured values. Actual costs will vary proportionally with document length. The full keyword dictionary, routing algorithm, cost model, and
analysis code are provided in the supplementary Jupyter notebook.


% ── 5. Experimental Results ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

All numbers below are computed from the full text of each document. The
released files \texttt{results/coverage\_by\_pair.csv},
\texttt{coverage\_by\_document.csv} and \texttt{coverage\_by\_control.csv}
regenerate every figure and table in this section, and
\texttt{code/run\_coverage.py} regenerates those files from the corpus.

\subsection{A correction to the earlier analysis of this corpus}
\label{sec:truncation}
An earlier analysis of this corpus extracted text with a fifteen-page cap. Seven
of the ten documents are longer than fifteen pages; the Maryland IT Security
Manual is $219$ pages, so its score was computed on roughly $7\%$ of its text.
Re-running the identical matching logic under that cap reproduces the earlier
corpus mean of $49.1\%$ exactly, which confirms the cause. Reading in full gives
$62.8\%$.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering\small
\caption{Effect of the fifteen-page cap. Documents at or under fifteen pages are
unaffected and are omitted.}
\label{tab:truncation}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Document & Pages & First 15\,pp & Full & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
MD Health Dept IT Security Policy & 122 & 54\% & 95\% & $+41$ \\
MD IT Security Manual & 219 & 59\% & 95\% & $+36$ \\
MD Local Gov Cybersecurity 2021 & 57 & 54\% & 77\% & $+23$ \\
MD Procurement Manual & 90 & 22\% & 36\% & $+14$ \\
MD Cybersecurity Council Report 2025 & 30 & 50\% & 63\% & $+13$ \\
MD MSDE Acceptable Use Policy 2024 & 17 & 40\% & 45\% & $+5$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{Corpus mean} & & \textbf{49.1\%} & \textbf{62.8\%} & $+13.7$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We report this rather than silently substituting the corrected figures. A
screening metric whose value moves by up to $41$ points depending on how much
of a document the extractor reads is telling the reader something about the
metric, and a practitioner deploying such a tool needs to know it.

\subsection{Coverage across the corpus}
Mean coverage is $62.8\%$ (SD $24.1$, range $36.4$ to $95.5$). Under the
threshold scheme of Section~\ref{sec:scoring}, three documents fall in the LOW
band ($\geq 80\%$), two in MEDIUM ($55$--$79\%$) and five in HIGH ($<55\%$).
Figure~\ref{fig:scores} plots full-text and truncated scores side by side.

Four of the twenty-two controls appear in all ten documents. The most
frequently absent, all of them rated HIGH risk in our control set, are access
control policy (PL-01, NIST AC-1) and incident reporting (PL-11, NIST IR-6) at
seven of ten documents each, encryption at rest (PL-15, NIST SC-28) also at
seven, and account management (PL-02, NIST AC-2) and audit review (PL-09, NIST
AU-6) at six. Section~\ref{sec:errors} shows that at least one of the PL-11
absences is a matcher failure rather than a policy gap.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig2_coverage_scores.pdf}
\caption{Control coverage per document, full text against the first fifteen
pages. Green, orange and red mark the LOW, MEDIUM and HIGH bands of
Section~\ref{sec:scoring}. The six documents with a visible grey bar are those
longer than fifteen pages.}
\label{fig:scores}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.86\linewidth]{fig6_length_confound.pdf}
\caption{Coverage against document length. The fitted line is a least-squares
fit on log word count, $r=0.690$, $r^2=0.476$.}
\label{fig:length}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What the coverage metric tracks}
\label{sec:whatitmeasures}
Three measurements bound the interpretation of the numbers above.

\emph{Length.} Coverage correlates with document length at $r=0.690$ against
log word count across the ten documents, so $r^2=0.476$: nearly half the
variance in the headline metric is explained by how long a document is rather
than by what it contains (Figure~\ref{fig:length}). The clearest illustration
is the Procurement Manual, which at $25{,}289$ words scores the same $36.4\%$ as
a $1{,}423$-word network security policy. A long document about the wrong
subject and a short document about the right one are not distinguished.

\emph{Negation.} Of the $138$ covered document-control pairs, $25$ ($18.1\%$)
contain a negation cue such as ``does not'', ``not required'' or ``except''
within $130$ characters of the matched keyword. Lexical matching cannot tell an
assertion from its denial, and roughly one covered pair in six sits next to
language that may reverse it.

\emph{Thinness of evidence.} $61$ of the $138$ covered pairs ($44.2\%$) are
covered on exactly one matched keyword, with a mean of $1.97$ keywords matched
out of $6.1$ available. Coverage is frequently a single word.

\subsection{Error analysis}
\label{sec:errors}
No expert-annotated ground truth exists for this corpus, so we report no
precision, recall or agreement statistic. What follows are cases identified by
inspecting matched keywords in context, each traceable to a released row.

\emph{A URL scored as transport encryption.} PL-14 (NIST SC-8) is scored
present in the Cybersecurity Council Report on the keyword \texttt{https},
matching a footnote citation to \texttt{umgc.edu}. The document is citing a web
page, not asserting a control.

\emph{Organisational roles scored as role-based access control.} PL-03 (NIST
AC-6) is scored present in two documents on \texttt{roles}, matching ``the
department responsible for roles: developing, maintaining, revising and
enforcing information security policy''.

\emph{A negated clause scored as personnel screening.} PL-18 (NIST PS-3) is
scored present in the IT Security Manual on \texttt{clearance}, matching
``procedures for the use of maintenance personnel that \emph{lack} appropriate
security clearances''.

\emph{An incident-reporting requirement scored as absent.} PL-11 (NIST IR-6) is
scored absent from the Judicial Information Security Policy, which in fact
requires ``notifying the JIS information security officer within two business
days of a security incident''. The keyword list contains ``incident reporting''
and ``notification requirement'' but not ``notify''. This single miss matters
beyond its cell: an inference that Maryland policies have not been updated to
reflect mandatory reporting timelines does not survive reading the document.

Three of these four are not keyword-selection mistakes. A URL is not a control,
a term can appear inside its own negation, and a concept can be expressed in
vocabulary absent from any finite list. Enlarging the lists fixes the fourth
case and worsens the first three.

\subsection{Projected orchestration cost}
\label{sec:costresults}
The figures in this subsection are token-price projections computed from
published per-token rates and estimated token counts, not measured spend. We
have not instrumented a run to record actual token usage, and a projection
should not be presented in the same register as a measurement. They are
reported here because the
routing design is part of the system this paper describes, and withholding the
arithmetic entirely would make that design harder to evaluate; they are
labelled throughout as projected.

Figure~\ref{fig:cost} compares per-task costs between the ProofLayer multi-LLM
strategy and a single \texttt{gpt-4o} baseline for a standard document audit.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig5_cost_analysis.pdf}
  \caption{(a) Per-task cost comparison between single-model (\texttt{gpt-4o} only) and
  ProofLayer multi-LLM routing. (b) ProofLayer model usage distribution
  across a typical audit session. Source: generated by the authors from the analysis pipeline described in Section~\ref{sec:corpus}.}
  \label{fig:cost}
\end{figure}

{\sloppy Table~\ref{tab:cost_breakdown} quantifies the cost reduction per task.
Routing document extraction and keyword mapping to \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} reduces
costs for those tasks by approximately 97\% compared to \texttt{gpt-4o}, since these
tasks require only token-level pattern recognition rather than complex reasoning.\par} Gap
analysis and report generation are retained on \texttt{gpt-4o}, as these tasks
require multi-hop reasoning over long policy contexts and structured output generation.
Long documents exceeding 15,000 characters are routed to \texttt{claude-sonnet},
which processes the full document context (${\sim}$6,700 input tokens) rather than
the 4,000-token window used in the single-model baseline. The lower per-token price
(\$0.003/1K vs.\ \$0.005/1K) offsets the higher token count, yielding equivalent
per-task cost with improved long-context coverage.
Overall, the multi-LLM strategy achieves an estimated 24\% cost reduction per audit,
driven entirely by routing extraction and mapping to \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}, reducing
the projected per-document cost from approximately \$0.113 to \$0.086.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Per-audit cost breakdown: ProofLayer vs.\ single \texttt{gpt-4o} baseline.}
\label{tab:cost_breakdown}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lllrrl}
\toprule
\textbf{Task} & \textbf{ProofLayer Model} & \textbf{Baseline} & \textbf{PL Cost} & \textbf{Base Cost} & \textbf{Savings} \\
\midrule
Doc. Extraction       & \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}   & \texttt{gpt-4o} & \$0.0005 & \$0.0160 & 96.6\% \\
Keyword Mapping       & \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}   & \texttt{gpt-4o} & \$0.0004 & \$0.0120 & 96.6\% \\
Gap Analysis          & \texttt{gpt-4o}        & \texttt{gpt-4o} & \$0.0255 & \$0.0255 & 0.0\%  \\
Report Generation     & \texttt{gpt-4o}        & \texttt{gpt-4o} & \$0.0245 & \$0.0245 & 0.0\%  \\
Long Doc ($>$15K)\textsuperscript{b} & \texttt{claude-sonnet} & \texttt{gpt-4o} & \$0.0351 & \$0.0350 & ---   \\
\midrule
\textbf{Total (typical audit)} & & & \$0.0860 & \$0.1130 & \textbf{23.9\%} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent\small\textsuperscript{b}Long-document routing directs \texttt{claude-sonnet} to process the full document context
(${\sim}$6,700 input tokens vs.\ ${\sim}$4,000 for the \texttt{gpt-4o} baseline). The lower per-token cost of
claude-sonnet offsets the higher token count, yielding equivalent per-task cost; the benefit of routing is
improved coverage, not cost reduction.

At scale, auditing all ten Maryland policy documents in this corpus costs approximately
\$0.86 under the ProofLayer strategy versus \$1.13 under single-model routing. For a
state government with hundreds of agency-level policies, this translates to substantial
operational savings while preserving higher-capability models for reasoning-intensive tasks.


