\section{Discussion}

The results in Section~5 surface three interconnected themes that we examine in
turn: the concrete remediation priorities implied by the identified gaps, the
methodological limitations of a keyword-based Stage 1 approach, and how ProofLayer
compares to recent LLM-based compliance systems in adjacent domains. Together,
these themes define the near-term improvement trajectory for ProofLayer and
illuminate the broader design space for automated government compliance tools.

\subsection{What this does and does not license}
\label{sec:license}
The corrected numbers do not support a remediation ranking for Maryland
agencies, and we withdraw the one an earlier version of this work offered. That
ranking was computed on truncated text; on full text the ordering of the most
frequently absent controls changes, and Section~\ref{sec:errors} shows that at
least one absence is a matcher failure rather than a policy gap. Recommending
where a state should spend its remediation budget on the strength of a keyword
count would be exactly the overreach this paper is arguing against.

What the results do license is narrower and, we think, more useful. Policy
documents in this corpus vary widely in the security vocabulary they contain,
from $36\%$ to $95\%$ of a twenty-two control set, and a large part of that
variation is length rather than substance. A screening tool built on keyword
presence will therefore rank a comprehensive manual above a focused policy
almost regardless of content, will mark roughly one in six matches next to
language that may negate it, and will miss requirements stated in vocabulary
its author did not anticipate. Those are quantified properties of a method that
is widely used as a first pass, and they are what a procurement officer
evaluating such a tool should be told.

\subsection{The language-model stage remains described, not evaluated}
\label{sec:stage2}
ProofLayer's Stage 2 language-model classifier is intended to address exactly
the failures catalogued in Section~\ref{sec:errors}: negation, context, synonymy
and control scope are the cases a lexical matcher cannot reach. The system
description in Section~\ref{sec:arch} specifies it, and the orchestration layer
routes to it.

It is not evaluated in this paper. We report no precision, recall, F1,
calibration or hallucination rate for it, because doing so requires an
expert-annotated ground truth for this corpus and none exists. We considered
generating labels with a language model and scoring the classifier against
them. That would produce numbers, and the numbers would be circular: a model
judging a model's output on a task where neither has been checked against a
human. Our companion work documents precisely this failure, in which model
agreement is mistaken for corroboration, and we are not going to commit it
here.

The honest statement is therefore that the failure modes measured in
Section~\ref{sec:errors} motivate a semantic stage, that ProofLayer implements
one, and that whether it helps is an open question this paper does not answer.
Establishing it requires independent annotation, which we identify as the
necessary next step rather than a limitation to be noted and passed over.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{Stage 2 is not validated, and no accuracy is claimed for it.} This study
evaluates only Stage 1 keyword-based mapping. The Stage 2 LLM gap analysis is
implemented and specified in Section~\ref{sec:arch} but has not been compared
against expert annotation, so its outputs carry no measured accuracy and should
not be read as such. The cost analysis in Section~\ref{sec:stage2} is likewise an
arithmetic projection over published vendor rates, not an observed saving.

We state the protocol that would settle this, both to make the gap concrete and
because the design constrains what a future result would mean. The unit of
annotation is the document-control pair, giving $10 \times 22 = 220$ judgments.
Each pair requires a ternary label: the control is addressed, the control is
absent, or the document is out of scope for that control. Two properties matter
for the result to be worth having. First, at least one annotator must be
independent of the system's authors, since an author labelling the output of
their own tool cannot establish independence; agreement between two annotators
should be reported as Cohen's $\kappa$ on a shared subset. Second, the labels must
be fixed before the Stage 2 outputs are inspected, or the comparison measures
post-hoc rationalization rather than accuracy. Language models may reasonably be
used to pre-annotate and to triage which pairs need human attention, but they
cannot supply the ground truth against which an LLM stage is judged, because that
measures agreement among models rather than correctness.

\textbf{No ground truth.} The control coverage scores and gap frequencies reported here are
derived solely from keyword presence. Without expert annotation, precision, recall, or
inter-rater agreement cannot be computed. A keyword can appear in a document in a context
that does not satisfy the corresponding control (e.g., ``encryption is currently
unavailable''), and a policy can fully satisfy a control without using the exact
terminology in the keyword dictionary. The results should be interpreted as indicators
of likely gaps rather than confirmed deficiencies.

\textbf{Keyword matching is not compliance verification.} Keyword presence is used as a
proxy for control coverage. The conclusions drawn about specific gaps reflect the output
of a pattern-matching heuristic and would require expert review to confirm.
Beyond false negatives and false positives in control detection, keyword matching cannot
distinguish between qualitatively different implementations of the same control. For
example, the PL-15 keyword set matches any occurrence of ``aes'' in a document
but cannot differentiate between modern authenticated encryption (e.g., AES-GCM) and
older cipher modes that may be vulnerable (e.g., AES-CBC without independent integrity
protection). A positive PL-15 match therefore indicates mention of AES-class encryption,
not necessarily a robust or compliant implementation.

\textbf{Small sample and absence of confidence intervals.} Ten documents represent a
single pass over a convenience corpus. No hypothesis testing or bootstrap confidence
intervals are reported. Results may not generalize beyond this specific corpus.

\textbf{Control dataset scope.} The 22-control dataset spans major NIST 800-53 families
but is not exhaustive. A full FedRAMP Moderate assessment requires over 300 controls.
Future work will expand the dataset and validate keyword coverage against annotated
policy corpora.

\textbf{Single-document analysis.} Each policy document is analyzed in isolation.
In practice, an agency's posture is determined by the union of all applicable policies;
a gap in one document may be addressed by another. Multi-document cross-referencing
is a planned ProofLayer feature.

\subsection{Comparison to Prior Approaches}

ComplianceNLP~\cite{compliancenlp_2026} achieves 87.7 F1 on gap detection in the
financial regulatory domain using knowledge-graph-augmented RAG. Our work is
complementary: ProofLayer targets government cybersecurity policies, applies a
two-stage keyword-plus-LLM pipeline, and contributes an empirical analysis of a real
policy corpus. Integrating knowledge-graph-based reasoning into ProofLayer's Stage 2
is a natural extension direction that could improve precision on borderline cases.


% ── 7. Conclusion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Conclusion}

This paper presented ProofLayer, an automated control coverage screening system that
assesses government cybersecurity policy documents against standardized control
frameworks (NIST 800-53, FedRAMP, CMMC). Applied to ten Maryland state and local
government policy documents, the keyword-based Stage 1 analysis gives a mean control
coverage of $62.8\%$ when documents are read in full. The most frequently absent
controls are access control policy (PL-01), incident reporting (PL-11) and encryption
at rest (PL-15), each missing from seven of ten documents, though
Section~\ref{sec:errors} shows at least one PL-11 absence to be a matcher failure
rather than a policy gap. Coverage correlates with document length at $r=0.690$, so
roughly half its variance across the corpus is attributable to length alone. The
orchestration cost saving is a token-price projection rather than measured spend
and is labelled as such throughout; no measured cost figure is claimed.

These results characterise what automated preliminary coverage screening does and
does not measure for resource-constrained government agencies. ProofLayer's
open-source pipeline, available at the project repository, provides a starting
point for state CISOs seeking a first pass over their cybersecurity policy
posture, subject to the limits set out above. Future work will validate Stage 2 LLM gap analysis against
expert-annotated ground truth, expand the control dataset to full FedRAMP Moderate
coverage, integrate knowledge-graph-augmented reasoning, and develop multi-document
cross-referencing to assess agency-level compliance posture across entire policy suites.


